WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:11.760
In order to fully understand monitoring,&nbsp;
first, one must have a better understanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:16.880
of what quality systems are, and what are&nbsp;
their purposes. Quality control systems are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:21.120
the operational techniques and activities&nbsp;
undertaken to verify the requirements for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:26.560
the quality of trial related activities.&nbsp;
Monitoring is a part of the QC activity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:31.120
Monitoring activities are undertaken&nbsp;
by the trial members themselves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:36.720
Audit is an inspection undertaken by personnel&nbsp;
independent of trial in accordance with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:43.840
protocol of the study’s standard operating&nbsp;
procedures or SOPs. It is part of a QA activity.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:51.280
A monitor is an individual employed by a&nbsp;
pharmaceutical or medical device manufacturer by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:57.280
a contract research organization, usually acting&nbsp;
on a sponsor’s behalf or by an academic Institute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:02.400
conducting clinical trials. Some of the&nbsp;
major responsibilities of the monitor are:

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:07.760
• assuring the protection of the rights,&nbsp;
safety and well-being of human study subjects.&nbsp;

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:14.080
• to analyze and evaluate clinical data.
• to ensure investigator and site compliance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:19.280
with the study drug protocol, overall&nbsp;
clinical objectives, FDA regulations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:24.400
ICH guidelines and good clinical practice.
• help in the study site selection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:26.400
process
• initiate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:32.240
and eventually close out clinical study sites
• to monitor the progress of clinical study sites&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:36.400
participating in a clinical study
• to ensure that protocol is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:41.600
followed and data is reported accurately
• to make certain that the scientific integrity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:47.200
of the data collected is protected and verified
• to assure that adverse events are correctly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:53.840
documented and reported, review all case report&nbsp;
forms and compare them to source documents

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.080
Auditing is the systematic and independent&nbsp;
examination of trial related activities in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:04.960
documents to determine whether the evaluated trial&nbsp;
related activities were conducted and whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:11.200
the data was recorded, analyzed and accurately&nbsp;
reported according to the protocol. The most cited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:16.480
clinical trial areas during audits include:
• adverse event review and reporting&nbsp;

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:21.040
• closure of study or lapse
• approvals while studying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:26.640
related activities which are still ongoing
• informed consent process and documentation&nbsp;

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:31.440
• drug or device accountability
• protocol adherence

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:39.600
Inspection is the act by a regulatory authority&nbsp;
or authorities of conducting an official review&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:44.720
of documents, facilities, records and any other&nbsp;
resources that are deemed by the authorities to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:50.000
be related to the clinical trial, and that may be&nbsp;
located at the site of the trial, at the sponsors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:55.280
or CRO’s facilities, or at other establishments&nbsp;
deemed appropriate by the regulatory authorities.

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:01.200
The inspections consist of site visits to the&nbsp;
organization and where appropriate, selected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:06.400
clinical investigator sites. The inspection&nbsp;
starts with an opening meeting where the lead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:11.760
inspector usually outlines the scope and purpose&nbsp;
of the inspection, confirms the inspection plan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:17.920
and introduces the inspectors upon completion of&nbsp;
the inspection process. If inspectors find issues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:24.080
related to the clinical trial process, certain&nbsp;
measures should be taken. Here are a few examples:&nbsp;

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:29.360
• protocol non-compliance
• inadequate or inaccurate records&nbsp;

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:33.920
• informed consent issues
• adverse event reporting

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:42.240
Some of the major differences&nbsp;
between an audit and inspection are:&nbsp;

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:48.800
• in audit, inspectors are employed by the&nbsp;
company who work for active clinical quality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:56.160
assurance function (e.g. sponsor, CRO and etc)
• an audit is done to ensure that the site&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:01.520
complies with protocol, SOP, GCP and&nbsp;
applicable regulatory requirement&nbsp;

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:07.120
• in inspection, inspectors are employed&nbsp;
by government through the agency of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:14.560
regulatory or competent authority (e.g. FDA)
• an inspection is done to ensure the trial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:27.520
related obligations and acceptability&nbsp;
of resultant clinical data is relevant

